# =============================================================================
# Cloudflare Pages _headers
#
# CACHING POLICY - READ BEFORE ADDING ANY Cache-Control RULE
#
# Rules in this file are applied BY PATH PATTERN, to every response at that
# path - including the single-page-app fallback HTML that Cloudflare Pages
# returns (with status 200) when no file exists at the requested path.
#
# That combination is a cache-poisoning trap. Verified live on production
# 2026-09-02:
#
#   GET /assets/index-DOESNOTEXIST.js
#   -> HTTP 200
#   -> content-type: text/html            (the SPA fallback, not JavaScript)
#   -> cache-control: public, max-age=31536000, immutable
#
# A browser or edge node that receives that response caches HTML in place of
# the app bundle for ONE YEAR and, because of `immutable`, never revalidates -
# not even on a manual reload. The page then renders pure white forever, with
# no error and no spinner. This is what broke the site on four phones between
# 2026-09-01 and 2026-09-02 while desktop kept working.
#
# Cloudflare's own guidance (developers.cloudflare.com/pages/configuration/
# serving-pages/): "In most situations, you should avoid setting up any custom
# caching on your site. Pages comes with built in caching defaults that are
# optimized for caching as much as possible, while providing the most up to
# date content." Pages keeps assets on its CDN until the next deployment and
# sends ETags, so browsers get 304 Not Modified instead of a re-download.
#
# THE RULE FOR THIS PROJECT:
#   Never set `immutable`.
#   Never set a long max-age on a path pattern that could also serve the SPA
#   fallback - which, with a `/*` fallback in _redirects, is every path.
#   A wrong answer must expire on its own. Self-healing beats fast.
#
# The JS and CSS cache rules that used to live here have been removed for that
# reason. Cloudflare Pages' defaults now apply to /assets/*: CDN-cached until
# the next deploy, ETag-revalidated in the browser.
# =============================================================================


# --- Security headers (all paths) -------------------------------------------
# static.cloudflareinsights.com is Cloudflare Web Analytics (free, privacy-
# preserving, no cookies). It is auto-injected by Pages and was previously
# blocked by this CSP, which is why the site had no analytics and threw two
# console errors on every page load.
#
# TWO hosts are needed, and this was confirmed by watching the live console
# rather than by reading the docs alone:
#   script-src  https://static.cloudflareinsights.com  - loads the beacon
#   connect-src https://cloudflareinsights.com         - where it SENDS the data
#
# Cloudflare's FAQ says an auto-injected beacon reports to your own domain, so
# connect-src 'self' should be enough. On this site it does not: the live
# console showed "Connecting to 'https://cloudflareinsights.com/cdn-cgi/rum'
# violates ... connect-src 'self' https://formspree.io". The script loaded and
# the upload was silently blocked - analytics would have looked switched on and
# collected nothing. Measured behaviour wins over documented behaviour.
# Source: developers.cloudflare.com/web-analytics/faq/ plus live observation
# 2026-09-03.
/*
  X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff
  X-Frame-Options: DENY
  X-XSS-Protection: 1; mode=block
  Referrer-Policy: strict-origin-when-cross-origin
  Permissions-Policy: camera=(), microphone=(), geolocation=(), payment=()
  Content-Security-Policy: default-src 'self'; script-src 'self' 'unsafe-inline' https://fonts.googleapis.com https://fonts.gstatic.com https://static.cloudflareinsights.com; style-src 'self' 'unsafe-inline' https://fonts.googleapis.com; font-src 'self' https://fonts.gstatic.com; img-src 'self' data: https:; connect-src 'self' https://formspree.io https://cloudflareinsights.com; frame-ancestors 'none'; base-uri 'self'; form-action 'self' https://formspree.io


# --- App code: never stale ---------------------------------------------------
# Added 2026-09-02, one push AFTER the main fix, because removing the bad rule
# was not enough on its own.
#
# With no rule here at all, the ZONE-LEVEL "Browser Cache TTL" setting in the
# Cloudflare dashboard filled the gap and served these paths as
# `max-age=14400` (four hours). Measured live, not assumed. That is far better
# than a year and it self-heals, but it still means a wrong answer could sit in
# a phone for four hours. The whole point of this fix is that a wrong answer
# cannot persist, so this rule closes that window to zero.
#
# Cost of max-age=0 here: none a visitor can feel. Cloudflare still serves the
# file from an edge server near them; the browser just asks "still current?"
# and gets a few-hundred-byte "yes" (HTTP 304) instead of assuming.
#
# CORRECTION, 2026-09-02, after this rule was deployed and measured:
# THIS RULE ALONE IS NOT ENOUGH. A first attempt assumed an explicit rule here
# would beat the Cloudflare dashboard setting. It does not, and the live site
# proved it - the deployed rule still came back as max-age=14400.
#
# 14400 seconds is four hours, which is Cloudflare's DEFAULT zone-wide
# "Browser Cache TTL" on every plan including Free. Cloudflare documents that
# it overrides the origin's Cache-Control whenever the origin's max-age is
# LOWER than that setting:
#
#   "By default, Cloudflare honors the cache expiration set in your Expires
#    and Cache-Control headers but overrides those headers if: the value of
#    the Expires or Cache-Control header from the origin web server is less
#    than the Browser Cache TTL Cloudflare setting."
#   - developers.cloudflare.com/cache/how-to/edge-browser-cache-ttl/
#
# That is why max-age=0 here became 14400, while /images/* at 86400 survived
# untouched: 86400 is higher than the setting, 0 is lower.
#
# THE OTHER HALF OF THIS FIX IS A DASHBOARD SETTING, NOT A FILE:
#   Cloudflare > Caching > Configuration > Browser Cache TTL
#   must be set to "Respect Existing Headers".
# Without it, this rule is silently raised to four hours. With it, this file
# is authoritative. Recorded as D-108.
/assets/*
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=0, must-revalidate


# --- Images: one day, always revalidated ------------------------------------
# Filenames here are NOT content-hashed, so a long cache would pin an outdated
# photo. `must-revalidate` with no `immutable` means a poisoned entry heals
# itself within 24 hours instead of persisting. Kept from the 2026-05-03 fix.
/images/*
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=86400, must-revalidate


# --- PDF downloads: same pattern as images ----------------------------------
/downloads/*
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=86400, must-revalidate


# --- JSON data (news-feed.json): never stale --------------------------------
/*.json
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=0, must-revalidate


# --- The HTML shell: never stale --------------------------------------------
# This is the file that names the current bundle hashes. It must never be
# cached, or a browser will keep asking for a bundle that no longer exists.
/
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=0, must-revalidate

/index.html
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=0, must-revalidate
